{
  "dom_id": "step-q136438",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "A particle starts moving from the point with position vector $(-i+2j)\\,\\text{km},$ with an acceleration of $(i-j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ and an initial velocity of $3j\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Find the distance from the particle to the origin after $4$ hours.",
  "worked_solution": "If a particle moves with constant acceleration $a,$ the position vector of the particle at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nr={r}_{0}+ut+\\frac{1}{2}a{t}^{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${r}_{0}$ is the initial position of the particle and $u$ is its initial velocity.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{0}=-i+2j,\\,u=3j,\\,a=i-j.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position vector of the particle after $t=4\\,\\text{h}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =(-i+2j)+(3j)(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(i-j)(4)^{2} \\\\  & =(-i+2j)+(12j)+8(i-j) \\\\  & =(-i+14j)+(8i-8j) \\\\  & =(7i+6j)\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the distance from the particle to the origin at this time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r| & =|7i+6j| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{85}\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
